{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/577720-demon-fallen-demon-descent-beasts-and-malakim","id":577720,"title":"demon fallen / demon descent / beasts and malakim","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Demon: The Descent"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-07-03T13:59:34-07:00","id":577720,"title":"demon fallen / demon descent / beasts and malakim","content":"During a crossover round an interesting thing popped up which I wanted to ask if it sounds likely.<br /> <br /> <br /> In demon the fallen god created malakim who fought the demons and thus turned the war around again. In owod the demons thought werewolves (who hated them automatically) wer these malakim. Also god was described as female in some fluff.<br /> <br /> In a crossover now with the translation guide and also with beast existing only one splat has an autoamtic dislike for demons and their kind (and angels): Beasts.<br /> <br /> Now: Is it probable that the dark mother mentioned in beast is god and the malakim from fallen legend would be the beasts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/914-ryu","name":"Ryu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-03T15:13:36-07:00","id":577828,"title":"","content":"Depending on the God-Machine's relationship to the God of the Fallen in your game, it's certainly an idea you could run with, sure. Have fun with it, it could lead to a lot of hooks and connections.<br /> <br /> I'd say it's an unlikely interpretation by the default foundation of the World of Darkness.<ul><li>The Begotten don't really overtly hunt the Unchained; they're much more focused with themselves and other phenomena in the World of Darkness. I'd say the default relationship between the two is more like cautiously giving one another a wide berth when there isn't a particular reason to directly involve themselves. Beasts don't <i>hate</i> the Unchained. They <i>distrust</i> them. They're an uncomfortable element that doesn't belong, but not necessarily an active enemy.</li> <li>The Begotten's relationship with the Unchained applies equally to loyal angels of the God-Machine, to the point where I'd argue beasts seem to conflate the two as one kind of being, or at least, two warring factions of one type of creature. If anything, the Unchained exist at an additional level of remove from the alien entity whose presence they distrust, less like Martian invaders and more like alien fauna accidentally introduced to the ecosystem when the invaders came.</li> <li>The Dark Mother's strongest associations are clearly with the Primordial Dream, the embodiment of the primal fears of mankind. She isn't nice; even her own Children think she's more likely to eat them than embrace them. If the Dark Mother is the Architect of the Universe, that says some bleak, bleak things about the true nature of the World of Darkness. The WoD is such a place that those bleak things aren't themselves improbable, but they do feel at odds with the melancholy but still hopeful portrayal of the Creator in Demon: the Fallen.</li> </ul><br /> Possibly the most ominous and disquieting conclusion that could be drawn from inferring the Dark Mother to be the Godhead of the World of Darkness is that means the Lancea et Sanctum's theology might be <i>correct</i>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":522,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","user":"Stupid Loserman","date":". 07-03-2015, 06:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-04T08:35:10-07:00","id":578796,"title":"","content":"I have no idea what the malakim are, but I will say this - <br /> <br /> The 2e nWoD does have a lot of yin-yang relationships here between God and Mother. In Requiem, we have CotC v. LeS. In Forsaken, we have Helios v. Luna. Unchained v. Begotten.<br /> <br /> There is absolutely a very strong leaning where (Sun) God supports the material/fallen world, forces the supernatural into shadows, and empowers humans as His favored creation-tool - this is the Status Quo of the God Machine. Meanwhile, the (Moon) Goddess/Dark Mother is the one who creates and champions the supernaturals. Under her influence, the Hisil is active, the strix and vamps thrive in their true nature, and the Children bond with each other.<br /> <br /> I don't know how this relates to the Awakened, the quashmal and Created, the Wyrd, the Inferno (Lucifuge and possibly Slashers), ghosts, or Du'at yet. But there's definitely ties with four of the current games that we can use.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-04T20:45:20-07:00","id":579411,"title":"","content":"Beasts and the unchained are supposedly not kin just because they really aren't kin, but there's no innate reason to apply that to the fallen, especially if you make the Dark Mother the creator of the angels.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-05T01:38:20-07:00","id":579643,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>StSword</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/577720-demon-fallen-demon-descent-beasts-and-malakim?p=579411#post579411\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Beasts and the unchained are supposedly not kin just because they really aren't kin, but there's no innate reason to apply that to the fallen, especially if you make the Dark Mother the creator of the angels.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not exactly though. The creator of angels created them but the demons (fallen) are perversions of what they originally were. Furthermore he had to create monsters (malakim) to be able to fight them effectively as else he would have lost the war against them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/914-ryu","name":"Ryu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-05T04:18:13-07:00","id":579696,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>StSword</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/577720-demon-fallen-demon-descent-beasts-and-malakim?p=579411#post579411\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Beasts and the unchained are supposedly not kin just because they really aren't kin, but there's no innate reason to apply that to the fallen, especially if you make the Dark Mother the creator of the angels.</div> </div> </div> </div> If the Dark Mother is creator of angels (oWoD God), then yes, that is very much a reason to apply kinship to the Fallen. The Fallen and all the monsters would literally share the same Mother-Creator.<br /> <br /> There are a lot of reasons why the Unchained aren't considered kin. One, the name <i>Unchained </i>is a big clue itself. You're unconnected to anything - all your relationships are a lie. Two, God-Machine is absolutely the Unchained's Creator, and there's really no way that it matches up with the Dark Mother's themes, they're too different, so any attempt to make the DM related to the GM would just do a disservice to both. Three, biomechanical horrors potentially from the future suggest lots of things unrelated to the Dark Mother. The list goes on.<br /> <br /> Personally, I think that equating God to the Dark Mother is a bit of a stretch - everything I said above about the God-Machine applies fairly well to DtF's God as well. The DM is all about the primordial chaos, while God is light and order. The two just don't line up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}